# CI GPU image for the aorta GPU test gate + workload regression smokes.
#
# Reproducibility: the ROCm PyTorch base is pinned by manifest digest. Extra
# dependencies are exact-pinned PyPI packages. The GPU pytest gate and the
# current workload smokes (gpu_smoke / inference / race) do not need TraceLens /
# Magpie, so those unpinned git installs from the general-purpose image are
# intentionally omitted here to keep the CI image reproducible and fast to build.
#
# The nightly sanitizer gate no longer builds RocJITsu from source (#350): it
# consumes prebuilt rj_waitcheck / rocjitsu_dbi_hooks artifacts published by
# ROCm/rocm-systems. The former source-build toolchain (cmake, ninja,
# libzstd-dev, g++-13 from the toolchain PPA, and a baked Findzstd.cmake shim)
# has therefore been removed from this image. hipcc (from the ROCm base) still
# builds the HIP repro fixtures and does not need any of those -- but on the ROCm
# 10 wheel base that is true only BECAUSE of the devel-symlink fixup below.
# Untouched, this base's hipcc compiles and then fails at the link step, and
# Proton fails to dlopen its rocprofiler backend. If you bump the digest,
# re-check that fixup; the two smoke tests beside it are there so the build
# tells you instead of the nightly sanitizer gate or the GPU gate telling you
# hours later.
#
# Base: rocm/pytorch ROCm 10.0, Ubuntu 26.04, Python 3.14, PyTorch 2.13.0
#
# Still the newest published combination on every axis at once -- the selection
# rule has not changed, only what satisfies it. This pin is the issue #383 stack
# flip, retargeted from 7.14 to ROCm 10.0 (released Aug 2026) because 10 landed
# before the flip did and supersedes it. It moves four axes at once, which is not
# a shortcut: the ROCm 10 line publishes no torch 2.10 image, so there is no
# smaller step off the old ROCm 7.2.4 / 24.04 / py3.12 / torch 2.10.0 pin.
#
# Python is now the same on both sides of CI: pyproject declares through 3.14 and
# the CPU matrix tests 3.10-3.14, so the GPU gate is no longer the axis lagging
# our own declared support -- it was pinned to 3.12 only because the classic ROCm
# line shipped nothing newer.
#
# What used to bound "newest ROCm" here was a 7.x argument -- 7.9-7.13 being the
# technology PREVIEW stream, 7.14 being the first wheel-based production release,
# and the move onto that wheel line being a deliberate separate step. All three
# are history: this pin IS that move, taken past 7.14 entirely. What bounds a
# future bump instead:
#
#   * ROCm 10 ships ONLY on TheRock, so the wheel layout is not a variant to opt
#     into any more. ROCm lives under site-packages and there is no /opt/rocm at
#     all -- verified on this digest, whose PATH carries no /opt/rocm/bin and
#     which sets no LD_LIBRARY_PATH. Issue #381 made discovery layout-agnostic;
#     the guard below is what re-proves it on every build.
#   * A bump WITHIN the ROCm 10 line is an ordinary digest bump. A bump that
#     changes the major is not: it drags Ubuntu, Python and PyTorch with it and
#     needs baselines re-blessed, so it stays a deliberate PR -- which is the
#     part of the old argument that survives.
#
# Pinned as tag@digest (not digest-only): the tag records which ROCm stream the
# digest is meant to come from, so a bump proposal that silently changes streams
# is visible in review (and anchors the docker Dependabot ecosystem to that
# stream for as long as .github/dependabot.yml enables it); the digest keeps the
# build reproducible. Resolved via:
#   docker buildx imagetools inspect rocm/pytorch:rocm10.0_ubuntu26.04_py3.14_pytorch_release_2.13.0
FROM rocm/pytorch:rocm10.0_ubuntu26.04_py3.14_pytorch_release_2.13.0@sha256:3174cb7061d94c427da96c0edef4adea28046fa3f3b2ff3948dc4e995665ff8c

USER root

# Require a READABLE ROCm install -- classic /opt/rocm or wheel (TheRock) --
# and fail the build if neither is found. See the script for the full rationale
# and for why it re-implements the resolver instead of importing it.
#
# Kept in the image rather than deleted after use: re-running it is the fastest
# way to explain a null rocm version on a runner. It is ~5 KB.
COPY rocm_layout_guard.py /usr/local/share/aorta/rocm_layout_guard.py
RUN python /usr/local/share/aorta/rocm_layout_guard.py

# Restore the bare `libfoo.so` devel symlinks this base does not ship.
#
# This base ships the ROCm RUNTIME wheels (`_rocm_sdk_core`,
# `_rocm_sdk_libraries`) but NOT `rocm[devel]` -- `rocm-sdk path --root` reports
# the devel package as absent. Runtime wheels carry only versioned sonames, so
# `libamdhip64.so.7` exists and the bare `libamdhip64.so` does not. 44 bare
# names are missing across the two trees: 27 under `_rocm_sdk_core/lib` and 17
# under `_rocm_sdk_libraries/lib`.
#
# TWO INDEPENDENT CONSUMERS need them, by two different mechanisms:
#
#   * hipcc hands the LINKER the bare name as an ABSOLUTE path, so every HIP
#     link fails:
#         ld.lld: error: cannot open .../_rocm_sdk_core/lib/libamdhip64.so:
#                 No such file or directory
#         clang++: error: linker command failed with exit code 1
#     hipcc itself resolves fine (/opt/venv/bin/hipcc) -- this is purely the
#     link step, which is why it survives a `command -v hipcc` check. It breaks
#     sanitizers-nightly.yml's fixture builds.
#   * Proton (the profiler shipped inside Triton) DLOPENS bare names at
#     runtime. `triton/_C/libproton.so` names five of them -- libamdhip64.so,
#     libhsa-runtime64.so, librocprofiler-sdk.so, libroctracer64.so,
#     libroctx64.so -- and Triton's own triton/profiler/__init__.py points it at
#     `_rocm_sdk_core/lib` through TRITON_ROCPROFILER_SDK_LIB_PATH, so
#     librocprofiler-sdk.so is opened by absolute path and the others by plain
#     loader search. It breaks tests/instrumentation/test_proton_smoke_gpu.py in
#     the GPU gate.
#
# ALL of them, rather than the two we have watched fail. Measured on this
# digest: with only libamdhip64.so linked, `proton.start()` dies with
#   RuntimeError: [PROTON] Could not load `librocprofiler-sdk.so`
# and adding just that one name moves the same failure to Proton's finalize:
#   RuntimeError: [PROTON] Could not load `libamdhip64.so`
# -- so naming libraries one at a time was already 2 of the 5 that a single
# consumer needs, and the third (libroctracer64.so) is named in that test file's
# own dlopen-failure skip marker.
#
# The earlier argument for the one-at-a-time shape was that fixing all of them
# papers over a base-image packaging gap with a wider blast radius than the
# failure warrants. It does not survive the evidence:
#   * It is not papering over. A bare-soname symlink layer is PRECISELY what
#     `rocm[devel]` installs; this reconstructs the absent package's link farm
#     rather than working around it, and it is the mechanism both consumers
#     were written against.
#   * The blast radius of one at a time is a red CI round per name on a shared
#     MI350 runner, hours after the fact. libamdhip64 surfaced through a
#     nightly fixture build and librocprofiler-sdk through the GPU gate, and
#     `workload regression (GPU, MI350)` has not yet run against this base at
#     all, so its consumers are still unexercised.
#   * The blast radius of all of them is small and measurable: a link is
#     created only where the versioned file already exists, so no library
#     appears in the image that was not already there, and all 44 were verified
#     to dlopen cleanly on this digest.
# Installing `rocm[devel]` is the true root-cause fix, but it needs network at
# image build time and adds size to an already ~52 GB image. It stays the thing
# to do when the base ships it -- at which point every link below is already
# present and this becomes a no-op.
#
# The lib directories are DERIVED, not hardcoded. A literal
# /opt/venv/lib/python3.14/site-packages/... path would rot silently the first
# time a base moves the venv or bumps Python -- exactly what issue #381 argued
# against. This re-implements the resolver's import-based discovery rather than
# importing aorta.instrumentation.rocm_paths, for the same reason
# rocm_layout_guard.py does: aorta is not installed at image build time. BOTH
# trees, because the two consumers above split across them: libamdhip64 is core,
# libhipblaslt is libraries.
#
# The soname is globbed rather than spelled, so a ROCm 10.x base that bumps
# `.so.7` to `.so.8` still works. `sort -V` rather than `sort`, so a tree that
# ever carries two majors side by side picks `.so.10` over `.so.9` instead of
# the lexicographic answer. The links are relative and created only when
# absent, so a future base that ships `rocm[devel]` makes this a no-op instead
# of a conflict.
#
# A non-wheel base is skipped, not failed: classic installs ship their own devel
# symlinks. The two smoke tests below are the real assertion either way -- they
# check the properties we actually depend on rather than the mechanism, so they
# stay honest if a future base fixes this upstream.
RUN set -eu; \
    libdirs="$(python -c 'import importlib.util, os; print(chr(32).join(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(s.origin), "lib") for s in (importlib.util.find_spec(m) for m in ("_rocm_sdk_core", "_rocm_sdk_libraries")) if s and s.origin))')"; \
    if [ -z "${libdirs}" ]; then \
      echo "no ROCm wheels (classic layout): skipping devel-symlink fixup"; \
    else \
      created=0; \
      for libdir in ${libdirs}; do \
        [ -d "${libdir}" ] || { echo "resolved ROCm lib dir does not exist: ${libdir}" >&2; exit 1; }; \
        cd "${libdir}"; \
        for versioned in *.so.*; do \
          [ -f "${versioned}" ] || continue; \
          bare="${versioned%%.so.*}.so"; \
          [ -e "${bare}" ] && continue; \
          newest="$(ls -1 "${bare}".* | sort -V | tail -n1)"; \
          ln -s "${newest}" "${bare}"; \
          created=$((created + 1)); \
          echo "created ${libdir}/${bare} -> ${newest}"; \
        done; \
      done; \
      echo "devel-symlink fixup: created ${created} bare sonames"; \
    fi

# Prove hipcc can link, at build time. Without the symlinks above this fails,
# and the failure would otherwise surface hours later as a broken nightly
# sanitizer fixture build rather than here. Same reasoning as the exact pins
# below: a broken image should surface during the build.
#
# gfx950 because that is what sanitizers-nightly.yml compiles for and this gate
# runs on MI350; no GPU is needed to compile and link for it. A base that could
# no longer target gfx950 SHOULD fail this build.
RUN set -eu; \
    printf '#include <hip/hip_runtime.h>\n__global__ void k(){}\nint main(){ hipLaunchKernelGGL(k, dim3(1), dim3(1), 0, 0); return 0; }\n' \
      > /tmp/hipcc_link_check.hip; \
    hipcc --offload-arch=gfx950 /tmp/hipcc_link_check.hip -o /tmp/hipcc_link_check; \
    rm -f /tmp/hipcc_link_check.hip /tmp/hipcc_link_check; \
    echo "hipcc link check: OK"

# And prove Proton can dlopen, at build time. The link check above covers only
# the hipcc half of the devel-symlink fixup; a missing bare soname that nothing
# LINKS against is invisible to it, which is how librocprofiler-sdk.so reached
# the GPU gate. This is that check for the runtime half.
#
# It asks libproton.so which ROCm sonames it names rather than repeating a list
# here. A hardcoded list would have to be re-derived by hand on every Triton
# bump, and a Triton that starts dlopening a sixth library would pass a stale
# list and fail in the gate -- the exact loop this fixup exists to break. The
# names sit in the binary as NUL-terminated strings, which is where they were
# found in the first place. An empty intersection is a build failure, not a
# pass: it means the extraction stopped working and the check is asserting
# nothing.
#
# BY BARE NAME, with the lib dirs on LD_LIBRARY_PATH, because that is how Proton
# opens them -- so this exercises the symlink and the loader search together.
# Appended, never prepended, mirroring the workflow export for the same reason.
# No GPU is needed: dlopen resolves and relocates the library without a device,
# and all 44 links were verified to load this way on a device-less container.
RUN set -eu; \
    libdirs="$(python -c 'import importlib.util, os; print(chr(32).join(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(s.origin), "lib") for s in (importlib.util.find_spec(m) for m in ("_rocm_sdk_core", "_rocm_sdk_libraries")) if s and s.origin))')"; \
    if [ -z "${libdirs}" ]; then \
      echo "no ROCm wheels (classic layout): skipping the proton dlopen check"; \
    else \
      printf '%s\n' \
        'import ctypes, glob, importlib.util, os, re, sys' \
        'spec = importlib.util.find_spec("triton")' \
        'if spec is None or not spec.origin:' \
        '    sys.exit("no triton installed, so proton cannot be smoke tested")' \
        'proton = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(spec.origin), "_C", "libproton.so")' \
        'with open(proton, "rb") as handle:' \
        '    blob = handle.read()' \
        'named = {m.decode() for m in re.findall(rb"lib[A-Za-z0-9_.+-]+\.so(?=\x00)", blob)}' \
        'shipped = sorted(' \
        '    name' \
        '    for name in named' \
        '    if any(glob.glob(os.path.join(d, name) + "*") for d in sys.argv[1:])' \
        ')' \
        'if not shipped:' \
        '    sys.exit(f"{proton} names no ROCm soname under {sys.argv[1:]}")' \
        'for name in shipped:' \
        '    ctypes.CDLL(name)' \
        'print("proton dlopen check: OK -- " + ", ".join(shipped))' \
        > /tmp/proton_dlopen_check.py; \
      LD_LIBRARY_PATH="${LD_LIBRARY_PATH:+${LD_LIBRARY_PATH}:}$(echo ${libdirs} | tr ' ' ':')" \
        python /tmp/proton_dlopen_check.py ${libdirs}; \
      rm -f /tmp/proton_dlopen_check.py; \
    fi

# NOTE for a future digest bump: hipcc-built binaries in this base carry NEITHER
# RPATH nor RUNPATH, so they need the ROCm lib dirs on LD_LIBRARY_PATH to RUN,
# even once they link. That is deliberately not baked in as an ENV here -- see
# the ROCM_HOME note at the bottom of this file for why this image does not
# assert ROCm paths, and docs/ci-testing-plan.md for the runtime side.
#
# CHECK THE BINARY, NOT THE IMAGE ENVIRONMENT. It is tempting to read the old
# 7.2.4 base as having been immune because it exported
# LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/rocm/lib; that is not what protected it. Its hipcc
# embedded DT_RUNPATH=/opt/rocm-7.2.4/lib in every binary, and a 7.2.4 fixture
# still gets past the loader with LD_LIBRARY_PATH scrubbed from its environment
# -- measured, both ways round. ROCm 10 lost the RUNPATH and the base
# LD_LIBRARY_PATH together, which is why the failure appeared here. So the
# question a bump has to answer is `readelf -d` on a hipcc-built fixture, not
# `printenv LD_LIBRARY_PATH` in the image: a base that restored the RUNPATH
# would be immune with no LD_LIBRARY_PATH at all, and one that only set
# LD_LIBRARY_PATH would still break any launch that does not inherit it.

# Exact pins for reproducible CI image builds; fail the build if these can't be
# installed (no `|| true`) so a broken image surfaces here, not at runtime.
#
# A Python bump is exactly when exact pins break, so both were re-checked against
# py3.14 before this base landed, and neither had to move with the interpreter:
# each is a pure-Python wheel (`py2.py3-none-any` / `py3-none-any`), so neither
# needs a per-interpreter build at all. The compiled work is all in seaborn's
# transitive deps -- numpy, pandas, matplotlib, contourpy, kiwisolver, pillow --
# and this base already ships every one of them at a version satisfying seaborn
# 0.13.2, so pip resolves this line without building anything. They publish cp314
# manylinux x86_64 wheels in any case.
RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir \
    "openpyxl==3.1.5" \
    "seaborn==0.13.2"

# Deliberately NO `ENV ROCM_HOME=/opt/rocm` here (issue #381).
#
# ROCM_HOME is an explicit operator override, so it ranks ABOVE autodetection in
# the resolver's lookup order. Setting it unconditionally asserts something this
# Dockerfile cannot know is true of its base. Measured on the 7.14 wheel image:
# with /opt/rocm entirely absent the claim is merely inert (the resolver
# validates the candidate and falls through to the wheel), but as soon as
# /opt/rocm exists in ANY form -- a compat stub with a bin/ in it is enough --
# it WINS over a correct wheel install and discovery lands on an empty tree.
#
# On the ROCm 10 base that stops being a hazard guarded against in advance and
# becomes the base's actual state: there is no /opt/rocm here at all, so the line
# would assert a path that does not exist. Keeping it out is now load-bearing
# rather than merely tidy.
#
# The old reason it cost nothing to drop -- that the classic base exported
# /opt/rocm/bin on PATH and /opt/rocm/lib on LD_LIBRARY_PATH itself, so the
# PATH/LD_LIBRARY_PATH lines that used to accompany it merely restated the
# image's own environment -- does not apply to this digest, which sets neither:
#   PATH=/opt/venv/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:...
#   LD_LIBRARY_PATH  (unset)
# There is nothing left to restate. Let discovery find the install under
# site-packages, which is the only place it is.

WORKDIR /workspace/aorta

CMD ["/bin/bash"]
